WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.569
-I want to congratulate you on
three years of "Sunday Today."

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:04.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you.
-And this is a show that

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
allows you to be in-depth,
conversational

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:09.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
with your subjects,
and you actually recently

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.111 size:75% position:38%
got the Tracy Morgan
treatment yourself.

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:13.413 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes. You've been on the show.
Fred's been on the show.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.081 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Fred's been on the show.
-Earlier adopters.

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:17.650
Thank you very much.
Tracy was a couple of weeks ago.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.552 line:92%
He invited us over to his house.

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:20.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Beautiful home.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.756 line:92%
Brought our entire crew inside.

00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:26.092
And, you know, Tracy's got his
quirks, and he's the best guy.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:28.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you go into the office
to sit down for the interview.

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:30.263 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we sit down,
and he said, "Willie,

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:31.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
did you notice anything
about my office?"

00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:33.633 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm like,
"It's kind of dark in here."

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he's like, "That's because
it's modeled directly

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:38.738 align:start size:94% position:6%
after Vito Corleone's office
in "The Godfather.'"

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:44.711
True story. Complete with the
shade. The blinds are down.

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:45.879 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And it's like to --

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:47.747 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, to the chair,
it's all the same.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:50.083 size:84% position:42%
And as we're talking --
we're sitting down --

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:51.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
there's a trophy case
between us.

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:55.321
And I look, and I'm like --
There's an Emmy in there.

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:56.623 line:92%
And I've done my homework.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:57.557 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm like,
"I know he was nominated.

00:00:57.557 --> 00:00:59.826 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not sure he won an Emmy."
And then I look down.

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:05.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's an Emmy, an Oscar,
and a Heisman Trophy.

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:09.369 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:11.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm like, "Tray,
what's going on with those?"

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:13.907
He goes, "Willie, it's America.
Money talks, baby."

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:17.410
But then I was like, "Is that
someone else's Heisman Trophy

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he bought
or just a replica?"

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.681 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You probably won't get
to the bottom of that story.

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:24.884
But I'm jealous, because I've
know Tracy for a very long time.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:27.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
And before the television show
"Shark Tank,"

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:29.656 line:92%
the shark tank I heard the most

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
was Tracy talked about
his shark tank.

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:33.827
Did you actually see the famous
Tracy Morgan shark tank?

00:01:33.827 --> 00:01:36.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think the number
is 20,000 gallons.

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:39.032 align:start size:94% position:6%
He has a 20,000-gallon
shark tank in his backyard.

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:42.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they're like --
-Just like in "The Godfather."

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:42.635 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Exactly. Yeah.

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:47.107
He's got those blacktip reef
sharks that were from Australia

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:49.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
they flew in and now live
in his backyard

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:50.176 size:88% position:44% line:92%
somewhere in New Jersey.

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:53.012
And we're sitting out there
talking. It's all on camera.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:53.813 line:92%
He's happy to talk about it.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:56.349
And he's like, "This is where
I come to get my peace of mind.

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:58.585
This is where I chill."
And he's like, "You know,

00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:01.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
and sometimes I come out here
to get freaky."

00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:03.389 line:92%
He's like -- And he goes,

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:08.027
"I'll say, 'Baby, tonight
I'm Jacques Cousteau.'"

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:10.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
He's just the best.

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He is the best person
to talk to.

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:15.368
In retrospect, I realize how
boring our conversation...

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:16.736 align:start size:94% position:6%
I apologize.
I apologize about it.

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:19.639
-Now the news guy's here to
talk about NATO expansion.

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:21.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's gonna be great.
-I do want to talk about

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:23.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
the crossover
between news and comedy.

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:26.880
There have been "Morning Joe"
sketches on "SNL" this year.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.181 size:75% position:38% line:92%
Mikey Day plays you.

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:30.984
Are you happy when you
see yourself on the show?

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:33.019
-I mean -- Well, there's a thing
where you wake up

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:35.321 size:84% position:42%
on a Sunday morning,
and you have 100 texts.

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:38.291
And you're like, "Uh-oh."
"You were on 'SNL' last night."

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:40.160 line:92%
And you're like, "Uh-oh."

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:40.860 size:78% position:39% line:92%
It's okay. It's fine.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'd like to get together
with Mike maybe over coffee.

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:45.298 align:start size:94% position:6%
Mikey and I could
talk through wardrobe.

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:46.366 align:start size:94% position:6%
We could talk through hair.
-Yeah.

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:49.802
-I feel like there's a little
mailing it in that's happening.

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:50.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Just for my part.

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:54.174
-I will say, yeah, the focus
is very much on Joe and Mika.

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:55.775 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're very --
You're a side character.

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.277 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right. And maybe that's okay.
Right?

00:02:57.277 --> 00:02:59.045
You're just -- You're not
the focus of the sketch.

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:01.648
-I would say don't work too hard
to get the crosshairs on you.

00:03:01.648 --> 00:03:05.084
-Cancel the coffee for tomorrow
downstairs? Okay, I will.

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:06.186 line:92%
-"Morning Joe" is obviously

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:09.322 align:start size:94% position:6%
a show that Donald Trump
has reacted to.

00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:11.191 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's very safe
to say that he --

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:12.959 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know
if he's still watching it,

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:15.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it's a show he has watched
with some amount

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:17.096 line:92%
of religiousness over the years.

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:20.867 size:84% position:42%
And the feedback loops
seems to be that you --

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:23.069
If he has something to tell you,
he just tells you.

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.772
Over the years, being in news,
have you talked to him much?

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:28.641
-Yeah, I mean, I haven't talked
to him in a couple of years,

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:32.579
since he really became president
and late in his campaign.

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:33.213 size:84% position:42% line:92%
But like you, probably,

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:35.949
if you live in New York
and you're on television,

00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:36.616 size:84% position:42% line:92%
whether you were, like,

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:39.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
the overnight weather anchor
on Channel 5,

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:40.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
he probably called you
at some point

00:03:40.987 --> 00:03:42.689 align:start size:94% position:6%
to talk through some things.
-Yes. Yeah.

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:45.325 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And the crazy thing about --
This is going back years.

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:47.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is Trump would call.
You'd get his assistant.

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:49.462 align:start size:94% position:6%
Rona would say,
"I have Mr. Trump for you."

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:51.698 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you'd go, "Okay."
And he would come through.

00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:54.033 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you're waiting
through 20 and 30 minutes

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.935 align:start size:94% position:6%
for the ask --
Or what's this about?

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:58.838
And it was never about anything.
It was pure gossip.

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:01.274 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, he was -- wanted to talk
about the media industry.

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:03.109
"Did you hear about this?
Did you hear about that?"

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:05.111 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he'd keep you on the line
for like 30 minutes.

00:04:05.111 --> 00:04:08.147
And he'd talk about politics,
and he'd talk about, you know,

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:10.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Did you hear
who's sleeping with who?"

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:11.517 line:92%
And all that kind of stuff.

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was just, like,
an old-school gossip guy

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.021 size:78% position:39%
who would call you up
on the phone at work.

00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:16.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then he's like,
"All right. That's all I got."

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:19.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
And hang up.
I don't know what happened.

00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:20.426 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Yeah. That's it.

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:22.829 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Your father was a newsman,
as well.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:25.064
Wrote for "The New York Times."
-"New York Times." Yeah.

00:04:25.064 --> 00:04:26.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And wrote a story about Trump
years ago.

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:28.801 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, so, he was a writer,
my dad was,

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.336 align:start size:94% position:6%
for "The New York Times"
in the '80s.

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:33.306 align:start size:94% position:6%
And in 1984, he wrote one of
the earliest profiles.

00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:35.174 line:92%
I think Trump was like 37.

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:37.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
And like, "Who is this
young brash guy?"

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:40.947
And so my dad wrote a piece for
"The New York Times" magazine

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:42.015 line:92%
that ended up on the cover.

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:45.652
And so Donald Trump views that
as his crowning achievement.

00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:47.754
Because what Donald Trump
wants more than anything

00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:49.455 align:start size:94% position:6%
is the approval of
"The New York Times."

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he shows that every day
on Twitter.

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:53.326 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, so, my dad
wrote the cover story.

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:56.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if you look right there,
that is my dad's story,

00:04:56.629 --> 00:05:00.233
hanging in the office where he's
putting in his office, I guess.

00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:02.135 line:92%
Something's happening there.

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:03.036 size:88% position:44% line:92%
And so Trump, I think --

00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:05.338 align:start size:94% position:6%
He could almost putt with
his tie, I want to point out.

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:09.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It is. It is.
It's a longer tie.

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:13.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, yeah, he's -- And I think
because of that piece,

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:14.514 line:92%
he's always had some weird --

00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:16.683 size:78% position:39%
My dad is Bill Geist,
so he's also William.

00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:19.085 size:78% position:39%
So maybe Trump thinks
I'm him or something.

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:21.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
So he's always been nice to me
because of that piece.

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:24.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the funny thing is,
all that mattered was that

00:05:24.157 --> 00:05:26.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
he was on the cover of
"New York Times" magazine.

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:28.394 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if you look inside
and you read it,

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:31.831
my dad was doing in 1984
what people are doing now,

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:33.166 line:92%
which is -- Trump tells my dad

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:34.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
he was number one
in his class at Wharton.

00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:37.837
And in the next sentence,
my dad says, "I called Wharton.

00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:39.372 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was not number one
in his class.

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:41.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
Didn't receive any honors."
You know what I mean?

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:42.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's, like,
that real-time fact check

00:05:42.809 --> 00:05:45.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
that is so in vogue
over the last couple of years.

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:47.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was happening
in that piece 35 years ago.

00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:49.382 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And yet none of us
paid attention.

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:50.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I know. I know.
It was all right there.

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:52.585 size:88% position:44%
-It was all right there.
-It was all right there.

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:55.455
-And we were like, "Who's on
the cover? Oh, I like him."

00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:57.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:01.260 size:88% position:44%
Congrats on three years.
Willie Geist, everybody.

